  "difficulty_rating": 1600,
  "explanation": "paginate slices `items[start:end + 1]`, one index past the intended end, so every page pulls in the first element of the next page too. For a page_size of 3, page 0 is items[0:4] (4 items, not 3) and page 1 is items[3:7], so index 3's value appears in both page 0 and page 1; the same overlap repeats between page 1 and page 2. Nothing crashes and nothing looks obviously wrong from a single page in isolation — the bug only becomes visible when you compare adjacent pages and notice the shared boundary element.",
  "prompt": "paginate is meant to slice out one page of `page_size` items. Step through render_pages and predict what each page contains.",
  "language": "python",
